  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "This variant gives Bob an in-band `pcid` that names how to interpret opaque payload bytes, so object type can survive transport without filenames and unsupported cases fail explicitly. That is a strong boundary-preserving answer to the scenario, but it answers the pressure by adding an envelope-carried protocol binding rather than making a bare CID codec alone distinguish pointer objects from raw bytes.",
    "strengths": [
      "`pcid` binds payload interpretation to transported bytes instead of local paths.",
      "Unknown-pCID handling preserves exact bytes and returns an explicit unsupported result instead of speculative misparse.",
      "Deterministic CBOR and mandatory signature slots support reproducible auditing and later verification."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The scenario's `CID codec identity alone` test is only partially met; the design relies on an enclosing envelope.",
      "Raw-chunk vs pointer-object semantics are delegated to the payload protocol, not defined by this specimen itself.",
      "The specimen is still draft and unfrozen, so long-term stability is not yet demonstrated."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "If payload bytes are detached from the envelope or re-addressed independently, type ambiguity can reappear.",
      "Opaque unknown payloads remain hard to audit unless the referenced protocol specs and handlers stay retrievable over time."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Should raw chunks and pointer objects have distinct payload `pcid` values, or should a higher-level CAS object format carry that distinction?",
      "When only a CID and bytes are available, how is the outer grid envelope itself identified for first-pass parsing?",
      "What process will mint and preserve durable `pcid`-to-spec bindings for 100-year auditability?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
